# Code owners: who is asked to review what.
#
# Two people, one of whom owns the repository, the settings, and most of the
# roles — so this file records who
# is *asked*, which is not the same claim as who is *able* to merge. GitHub will
# request a review from the owner on almost every pull request including their own,
# and nothing here stops that owner merging it. Anyone weighing a contribution to
# SWP-1 should read that as the fact it is: the review check on the protocol and
# the documentation is, right now, the same pair of eyes that wrote them.
#
# The split by path is the reason this file exists at all. The two kinds of
# judgement this project needs are not the same kind: a change to the protocol, the
# evidence ladder or a documented claim has to be read by somebody who will answer
# for the claim, and a change to a grammar table does not need that, but does need
# the adapter's tests to pass.

# Everything, by default: the protocol, the evidence ladder, the release pipeline.
*                                       @theaaqibjavaid

# Language adapters and their fixtures — the surface a second maintainer can own
# without holding the claims, because an adapter is checked by its own tests.
/crates/swp-adapters/                   @theaaqibjavaid @justin-coders

# The documentation pages and the suite that re-runs them. The wording of a claim
# stays with the owner; the mechanics of the transcript test do not.
/docs/                                  @theaaqibjavaid
/crates/swp-test-suite/tests/docs/      @theaaqibjavaid

# Legal records. A signature is the grant somebody makes to the project, so the
# person accepting a grant cannot be the person asserting one: this path is the
# owner's alone, which is also why the owner's own CLA cannot be self-certified by
# a contributor editing the directory.
/CLA.md                                 @theaaqibjavaid
/CLA/signatures/                        @theaaqibjavaid

# What the project promises a sponsor, and the copy that promises it. A tier is a
# commercial commitment made in the name of the entity that takes the money, so it
# is not a review a second maintainer can approve on the owner's behalf.
/SPONSORS.md                            @theaaqibjavaid
